    "content": "<!-- wp:cil/page-head {\"eyebrow\":\"Condition · Lichen sclerosis\",\"title\":\"BXO / Lichen Sclerosis in Men\",\"lede\":\"BXO (balanitis xerotica obliterans), also known as male genital lichen sclerosis, is a chronic inflammatory condition that can cause whitening, scarring and progressive tightening of the foreskin.\",\"reviewedBy\":\"\\u003ca href=\\u0022https://circumcision-london-wp.vercel.app/team/#haidar\\u0022\\u003eDr Haidar Al-Ali, BDS, MFDS RCPS (Glasg)\\u003c/a\\u003e, September 2026\",\"crumbs\":[{\"label\":\"Reasons\",\"href\":\"https://circumcision-london-wp.vercel.app/conditions/phimosis/\"},{\"label\":\"BXO\",\"href\":\"\"}]} /-->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/section {\"size\":\"section-sm\",\"band\":\"\",\"wrap\":\"wrap\"} --><section class=\"wp-block-cil-section section-sm\"><div class=\"wrap\"><!-- wp:cil/split {\"reverse\":false} --><div class=\"wp-block-cil-split split\"><!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"body-text\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-group body-text\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>As the foreskin becomes less elastic, it can become increasingly difficult or painful to retract. Some patients develop cracking, bleeding, painful erections or a tight scarred ring around the foreskin opening.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>At Beverley Clinic in North-West London, we provide circumcision for children, teenagers and adults with BXO / lichen sclerosis affecting the foreskin.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>When circumcision is being performed for BXO, it is particularly important to recognise the abnormal scarred foreskin so that the affected foreskin is appropriately removed.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:group --><!-- wp:cil/spec-panel {\"eyebrow\":\"BXO at a glance\",\"rows\":[{\"k\":\"What is it?\",\"v\":\"A chronic inflammatory and scarring condition affecting the foreskin and sometimes the glans.\"},{\"k\":\"What does it look like?\",\"v\":\"The foreskin may appear white or pale, thickened, scarred or less elastic.\"},{\"k\":\"What problems can it cause?\",\"v\":\"Progressive phimosis, painful retraction, cracking, bleeding, painful erections and sometimes urinary problems.\"},{\"k\":\"Who can develop it?\",\"v\":\"BXO can occur in both children and adults.\"},{\"k\":\"What treatment does Beverley Clinic provide?\",\"v\":\"Our clinic provides circumcision for BXO affecting the foreskin.\"},{\"k\":\"Why is assessment important?\",\"v\":\"The extent and position of the scarred tissue needs to be recognised before the circumcision is planned.\"}],\"note\":\"An adult circumcision involving a medical or foreskin problem is £1,080. See our \\u003ca href=\\u0022https://circumcision-london-wp.vercel.app/prices/\\u0022 style=\\u0022color:var(\\u002d\\u002dblue-deep)\\u0022\\u003eprice list\\u003c/a\\u003e.\"} /--></div><!-- /wp:cil/split --></div></section><!-- /wp:cil/section -->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/section {\"size\":\"section\",\"band\":\"bg-card edge\",\"wrap\":\"wrap\",\"anchor\":\"what-is-bxo\"} --><section class=\"wp-block-cil-section section bg-card edge\" id=\"what-is-bxo\"><div class=\"wrap\"><!-- wp:cil/section-head {\"eyebrow\":\"Understanding BXO\",\"heading\":\"What is BXO?\",\"display\":\"d-2\",\"lede\":\"BXO stands for balanitis xerotica obliterans.\"} /--><!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"body-text\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-group body-text\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>It is the term commonly used when lichen sclerosis affects the male genital area, particularly the foreskin and glans.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You may see the condition referred to elsewhere as lichen sclerosus, which is an alternative spelling commonly used in medical literature.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>BXO can gradually change the structure of the foreskin. Healthy foreskin is normally soft and elastic, whereas foreskin affected by BXO can become pale, thickened, fragile, scarred and less flexible.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>As this progresses, the foreskin opening can narrow and eventually become too tight to retract over the head of the penis.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:group --></div></section><!-- /wp:cil/section -->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/section {\"size\":\"section\",\"band\":\"\",\"wrap\":\"wrap\",\"anchor\":\"appearance-and-symptoms\"} --><section class=\"wp-block-cil-section section\" id=\"appearance-and-symptoms\"><div class=\"wrap\"><!-- wp:cil/split {\"reverse\":false} --><div class=\"wp-block-cil-split split\"><!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"body-text\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-group body-text\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2,\"className\":\"wp-block-heading display d-2\"} --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading display d-2\">What does BXO look like?</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The appearance varies between patients.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Typical changes can include:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"cil-checklist\"} --><ul class=\"cil-checklist wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A white or pale area of foreskin</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A white ring around the foreskin opening</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Thickened or hardened skin</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A tight scarred foreskin</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Loss of normal skin elasticity</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Small cracks or splits</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Bleeding following retraction</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Red or inflamed areas</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Changes affecting the glans</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Narrowing around the urinary opening in more extensive disease</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Some patients initially think they simply have a tight foreskin.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>On examination, however, the white or scarred ring characteristic of BXO may be visible.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:group --><!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"body-text\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-group body-text\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2,\"className\":\"wp-block-heading display d-2\"} --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading display d-2\">What are the symptoms of BXO?</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>BXO does not cause identical symptoms in every patient.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Symptoms can include:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"cil-checklist\"} --><ul class=\"cil-checklist wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Increasing difficulty retracting the foreskin</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Phimosis</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Pain when retracting the foreskin</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A tight or constricting sensation</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Cracking or splitting of the foreskin</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Bleeding from small tears</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Painful erections</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Discomfort during sexual activity</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Recurrent inflammation</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Difficulty cleaning underneath the foreskin</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Changes in the appearance of the foreskin</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Problems with the urinary stream if the condition affects the urinary opening</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The condition can develop gradually, so some patients do not appreciate how much their foreskin has changed until retraction becomes significantly restricted.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:group --></div><!-- /wp:cil/split --></div></section><!-- /wp:cil/section -->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/section {\"size\":\"section\",\"band\":\"bg-warm\",\"wrap\":\"wrap\",\"anchor\":\"phimosis-and-cycle\"} --><section class=\"wp-block-cil-section section bg-warm\" id=\"phimosis-and-cycle\"><div class=\"wrap\"><!-- wp:cil/section-head {\"eyebrow\":\"Progressive Tightness\",\"heading\":\"BXO and phimosis\",\"display\":\"d-2\",\"lede\":\"BXO is an important cause of pathological phimosis.\"} /--><!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"body-text\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-group body-text\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Phimosis means that the foreskin is too tight to retract comfortably over the glans.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>With BXO, chronic inflammation can cause the foreskin to lose elasticity and develop a firm scarred ring.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A patient may previously have been able to retract his foreskin normally but notice that it becomes progressively tighter.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This is different from the normal non-retractable foreskin seen in babies and many younger boys.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p><a href=\"https://circumcision-london-wp.vercel.app/conditions/phimosis/\">Learn more about phimosis →</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:group --><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3,\"className\":\"wp-block-heading display d-3\"} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading display d-3\">The cycle of tightness, cracking and scarring</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>One of the problems we see with a scarred foreskin is a cycle of:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"cil-cycle-flow\"} --><ul class=\"cil-cycle-flow wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li><span class=\"cycle-title\">Tightness</span></li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><span class=\"cycle-title\">Attempted retraction</span></li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><span class=\"cycle-title\">Cracking or micro-tears</span></li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><span class=\"cycle-title\">Healing and further scarring</span></li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li><span class=\"cycle-title\">Increased tightness</span></li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --><!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"body-text\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-group body-text\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>As the foreskin becomes less elastic, attempting to retract it can produce further splitting.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The patient may then notice that the foreskin becomes increasingly difficult to retract despite repeatedly trying to loosen it.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:group --><!-- wp:cil/callout {\"title\":\"Clinical advice\",\"text\":\"If the foreskin is visibly scarred, white or repeatedly cracking, do not forcibly retract it.\"} /--></div></section><!-- /wp:cil/section -->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/section {\"size\":\"section\",\"band\":\"bg-card edge\",\"wrap\":\"wrap\",\"anchor\":\"children-and-adults\"} --><section class=\"wp-block-cil-section section bg-card edge\" id=\"children-and-adults\"><div class=\"wrap\"><!-- wp:cil/split {\"reverse\":false} --><div class=\"wp-block-cil-split split\"><!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"body-text\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-group body-text\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2,\"className\":\"wp-block-heading display d-2\"} --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading display d-2\">Can children get BXO?</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>BXO can occur in children as well as teenagers and adults.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A non-retractable foreskin alone is very common in younger boys and does not mean that a child has BXO.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The concern is different when there are features such as:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"cil-checklist\"} --><ul class=\"cil-checklist wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A clearly scarred foreskin</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A white or pale tight ring</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Progressive rather than developmental tightness</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Repeated cracking</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Significant inflammation</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Pain</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Urinary symptoms</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>We examine the foreskin before circumcision because it is important to distinguish a normally developing foreskin from one affected by pathological scarring.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p><a href=\"https://circumcision-london-wp.vercel.app/children/\">Learn more about circumcision for children →</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:group --><!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"body-text\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-group body-text\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2,\"className\":\"wp-block-heading display d-2\"} --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading display d-2\">Can adults develop BXO?</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Yes.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Some adults develop increasing foreskin tightness despite previously having a normally retractable foreskin.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A man may initially notice that retraction becomes uncomfortable during erections. Later, the foreskin may begin cracking or become difficult to retract even when the penis is soft.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>A new tight foreskin in an adult, particularly where there is whitening or scarring, should be medically assessed.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:group --></div><!-- /wp:cil/split --></div></section><!-- /wp:cil/section -->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/section {\"size\":\"section\",\"band\":\"\",\"wrap\":\"wrap\",\"anchor\":\"causes-and-diagnosis\"} --><section class=\"wp-block-cil-section section\" id=\"causes-and-diagnosis\"><div class=\"wrap\"><!-- wp:cil/section-head {\"eyebrow\":\"Clinical Facts\",\"heading\":\"Causes and diagnosis of BXO\",\"display\":\"d-2\"} /--><!-- wp:cil/info-cards {\"columns\":\"g-3\",\"headingLevel\":3,\"items\":[{\"eyebrow\":\"Transmission\",\"title\":\"Is BXO contagious?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eNo.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eBXO / lichen sclerosis is not a sexually transmitted infection and it is not contagious.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eYou cannot pass BXO to a partner through sexual contact.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eIt is also not simply caused by poor personal hygiene.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"},{\"eyebrow\":\"Aetiology\",\"title\":\"What causes BXO?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eThe exact cause is not completely understood.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eIt is regarded as a chronic inflammatory skin condition, and immune-system factors are thought to be involved.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWhat matters clinically is recognising the characteristic changes and determining how much of the foreskin and surrounding penile tissue appears to be affected.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"},{\"eyebrow\":\"Clinical assessment\",\"title\":\"How is BXO diagnosed?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eBXO is often suspected from the patient's history and the appearance of the foreskin during examination.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eNot every white patch on the penis is BXO. Other skin conditions can produce changes in colour or appearance, which is why a persistent or unusual penile lesion should not simply be self-diagnosed from photographs online.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"}]} /--><!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"body-text\",\"style\":{\"spacing\":{\"margin\":{\"top\":\"32px\"}}}} --><div class=\"wp-block-group body-text\" style=\"margin-top:32px\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>During examination, we look for features such as:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"cil-checklist\"} --><ul class=\"cil-checklist wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Whitening or pale scar tissue</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A tight fibrous ring</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Loss of elasticity</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Cracking or splitting</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Thickening</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Associated phimosis</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Changes involving the glans</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Changes around the urinary opening</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --></div><!-- /wp:group --></div></section><!-- /wp:cil/section -->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/section {\"size\":\"section\",\"band\":\"bg-warm\",\"wrap\":\"wrap\",\"anchor\":\"circumcision-for-bxo\"} --><section class=\"wp-block-cil-section section bg-warm\" id=\"circumcision-for-bxo\"><div class=\"wrap\"><!-- wp:cil/section-head {\"eyebrow\":\"Surgical Treatment\",\"heading\":\"Circumcision for BXO\",\"display\":\"d-2\"} /--><!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"body-text\",\"style\":{\"spacing\":{\"margin\":{\"bottom\":\"40px\"}}}} --><div class=\"wp-block-group body-text\" style=\"margin-bottom:40px\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The treatment we provide at Beverley Clinic for BXO affecting the foreskin is circumcision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Circumcision removes the foreskin, including the tight and scarred foreskin responsible for the phimosis.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For BXO, the procedure needs to be planned with particular attention to where the diseased or scarred tissue is located.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This is different from approaching circumcision as a purely cosmetic procedure on otherwise healthy foreskin.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The aim is to adequately remove the problematic foreskin rather than leave a significant tight or diseased ring behind.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:group --><!-- wp:cil/split {\"reverse\":false} --><div class=\"wp-block-cil-split split\"><!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"body-text\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-group body-text\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2,\"className\":\"wp-block-heading display d-2\"} --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading display d-2\">Why does the style of circumcision matter with BXO?</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>When circumcision is being performed for religious, cultural or personal reasons on healthy foreskin, there may be more flexibility regarding how the foreskin is distributed.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>BXO is different.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>There is an underlying scarring condition, so our priority is to identify the abnormal foreskin and make sure that the circumcision appropriately addresses it.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Before proceeding we consider:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"cil-checklist\"} --><ul class=\"cil-checklist wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>The position of the scarred ring</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>How much foreskin appears affected</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>The mobility of the surrounding penile skin</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Whether the glans appears affected</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>The urinary opening</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>The frenulum</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Whether there is a buried or retractile penis</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Any previous penile surgery</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The circumcision is then planned according to the individual anatomy and the extent of the foreskin problem.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:group --><!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"body-text\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-group body-text\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2,\"className\":\"wp-block-heading display d-2\"} --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading display d-2\">What happens during circumcision for BXO?</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>For suitable adults at Beverley Clinic, circumcision is normally performed under local anaesthetic.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The penis and foreskin are examined before the procedure and the scarred area is identified.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>We commonly use a forceps-guided circumcision technique with thermal cautery.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The affected foreskin is removed and thermal cautery helps control bleeding.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Depending on the individual wound, the skin may be closed using dissolvable stitches, medical skin glue, a combination of the two, or occasionally neither.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You receive detailed aftercare instructions before leaving the clinic.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p><a href=\"https://circumcision-london-wp.vercel.app/adults/\">Learn more about adult circumcision →</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:group --></div><!-- /wp:cil/split --></div></section><!-- /wp:cil/section -->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/section {\"size\":\"section\",\"band\":\"bg-card edge\",\"wrap\":\"wrap\",\"anchor\":\"anatomy-and-risks\"} --><section class=\"wp-block-cil-section section bg-card edge\" id=\"anatomy-and-risks\"><div class=\"wrap\"><!-- wp:cil/section-head {\"eyebrow\":\"Anatomical Considerations\",\"heading\":\"Disease extent and clinical precautions\",\"display\":\"d-2\"} /--><!-- wp:cil/info-cards {\"columns\":\"g-3\",\"headingLevel\":3,\"items\":[{\"eyebrow\":\"Ongoing disease\",\"title\":\"Can BXO continue after circumcision?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eCircumcision removes the affected foreskin and can successfully treat BXO that is limited to the foreskin.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eHowever, BXO can sometimes involve the glans or urinary opening as well as the foreskin.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eCircumcision therefore does not mean that changes elsewhere on the penis should subsequently be ignored.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eIf there are persistent white areas, changes around the urinary opening, difficulty passing urine or another abnormal area after healing, further medical assessment may be required.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"},{\"eyebrow\":\"Meatal involvement\",\"title\":\"BXO and the urinary opening\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eIn some patients, lichen sclerosis can affect the meatus, which is the opening at the tip of the penis through which urine passes.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eScarring around this area can cause the opening to become narrower.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eCircumcision removes the foreskin but does not itself treat narrowing further inside the urinary opening or urethra. Patients with suspected meatal or urethral involvement may require assessment by an appropriate urology service.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003cstrong\\u003eIf you cannot pass urine at all, seek urgent medical attention.\\u003c/strong\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\"},{\"eyebrow\":\"Clinical awareness\",\"title\":\"Is BXO associated with penile cancer?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eThere is an association between genital lichen sclerosis and an increased risk of penile cancer, although the overall risk remains low.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThis does not mean that a patient with BXO has cancer or will develop cancer. It does mean that persistent abnormal changes should not simply be ignored.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThese findings require appropriate medical assessment rather than simply booking a routine circumcision without mentioning them.\\u003c/p\\u003e\"}]} /--><!-- wp:cil/split {\"reverse\":false} --><div class=\"wp-block-cil-split split\"><!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"body-text\",\"style\":{\"spacing\":{\"margin\":{\"top\":\"32px\"}}}} --><div class=\"wp-block-group body-text\" style=\"margin-top:32px\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Symptoms of meatal involvement can include:</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"cil-checklist\"} --><ul class=\"cil-checklist wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A weaker urinary stream</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Spraying of urine</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Taking longer to empty the bladder</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Straining to pass urine</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Pain or discomfort during urination</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Tell us if you have urinary symptoms when you contact the clinic.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:group --><!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"body-text\",\"style\":{\"spacing\":{\"margin\":{\"top\":\"32px\"}}}} --><div class=\"wp-block-group body-text\" style=\"margin-top:32px\"><!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Seek medical assessment if you notice:</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"cil-checklist\"} --><ul class=\"cil-checklist wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A persistent ulcer or sore</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A lump</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>An area that repeatedly bleeds</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A persistent thickened area</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>A new or changing lesion</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>An abnormal area that does not heal</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --></div><!-- /wp:group --></div><!-- /wp:cil/split --></div></section><!-- /wp:cil/section -->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/section {\"size\":\"section\",\"band\":\"\",\"wrap\":\"wrap\",\"anchor\":\"differential-comparisons\"} --><section class=\"wp-block-cil-section section\" id=\"differential-comparisons\"><div class=\"wrap\"><!-- wp:cil/section-head {\"eyebrow\":\"Clinical Differentiation\",\"heading\":\"Distinguishing BXO from other foreskin conditions\",\"display\":\"d-2\"} /--><!-- wp:cil/info-cards {\"columns\":\"g-2\",\"headingLevel\":3,\"items\":[{\"eyebrow\":\"Differential diagnosis\",\"title\":\"BXO or balanitis – what is the difference?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003eBalanitis describes inflammation of the glans and can occur for many different reasons.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eBXO / lichen sclerosis is a chronic inflammatory and scarring condition that can progressively alter the structure and elasticity of the foreskin.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eA patient with BXO can also experience inflammation, so the two terms are not mutually exclusive.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eThe presence of whitening, significant scarring and progressive phimosis makes BXO particularly important to consider.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\u0022https://circumcision-london-wp.vercel.app/conditions/balanitis/\\u0022\\u003eLearn more about recurrent balanitis →\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\"},{\"eyebrow\":\"Differential diagnosis\",\"title\":\"BXO or ordinary phimosis – what is the difference?\",\"html\":\"\\u003cp\\u003ePhimosis simply describes a foreskin that is too tight to retract.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eBXO is one possible cause of pathological phimosis.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eA tight foreskin without significant scarring may look quite different from a foreskin affected by BXO.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eWith BXO, we may see a distinctive pale or white scarred ring, thickening, loss of elasticity and repeated cracking.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003eRecognising the difference matters when planning the circumcision because the abnormal scarred tissue needs to be appropriately treated.\\u003c/p\\u003e\\u003cp\\u003e\\u003ca href=\\u0022https://circumcision-london-wp.vercel.app/conditions/phimosis/\\u0022\\u003eLearn more about phimosis →\\u003c/a\\u003e\\u003c/p\\u003e\"}]} /--><!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"wp-block-group is-style-cil-card body-text\",\"style\":{\"spacing\":{\"margin\":{\"top\":\"36px\"}}}} --><div class=\"wp-block-group is-style-cil-card body-text\" style=\"margin-top:36px\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3,\"className\":\"wp-block-heading display d-3\"} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading display d-3\">From our clinical experience</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Patients frequently contact us saying that they have a “tight foreskin”, without realising that the foreskin itself has become scarred.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>On examination, some of these patients have a clearly defined white, inelastic band of tissue rather than simple tightness.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This distinction matters.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>When we circumcise a patient with BXO, our attention is not simply on how much foreskin to remove. We identify the position and extent of the abnormal scarred foreskin so that the procedure addresses the medical problem for which the patient came to us.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:group --></div></section><!-- /wp:cil/section -->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/section {\"size\":\"section\",\"band\":\"bg-warm\",\"wrap\":\"wrap\",\"anchor\":\"recovery-and-risks\"} --><section class=\"wp-block-cil-section section bg-warm\" id=\"recovery-and-risks\"><div class=\"wrap\"><!-- wp:cil/split {\"reverse\":false} --><div class=\"wp-block-cil-split split\"><!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"body-text\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-group body-text\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2,\"className\":\"wp-block-heading display d-2\"} --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading display d-2\">Recovery after circumcision for BXO</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Some swelling, bruising, tenderness and sensitivity are expected following circumcision.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If severe BXO or phimosis meant that the glans was rarely exposed before surgery, the newly exposed glans can initially feel particularly sensitive.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>This generally becomes easier as healing progresses and the patient becomes accustomed to the glans being exposed.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>The early appearance is not the final result. Swelling reduces and the circumcision scar continues to settle over time.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>We provide detailed written aftercare and follow-up support during healing.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p><a href=\"https://circumcision-london-wp.vercel.app/aftercare/\">Read our circumcision aftercare guide →</a></p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2,\"className\":\"wp-block-heading display d-2\"} --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading display d-2\">When can I have sex after circumcision for BXO?</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>At Beverley Clinic, we generally advise avoiding sexual intercourse and masturbation for at least two weeks and until the wound is sufficiently healed.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Healing varies between patients.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If the wound has not healed sufficiently at two weeks, or sexual activity would place tension on the wound, you should wait longer.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Follow the individual aftercare instructions provided following your procedure.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:group --><!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"body-text\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-group body-text\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2,\"className\":\"wp-block-heading display d-2\"} --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading display d-2\">What are the risks of circumcision?</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Circumcision is a surgical procedure and complications can occur.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Potential risks include:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"cil-checklist\"} --><ul class=\"cil-checklist wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Bleeding</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Infection</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Swelling and bruising</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Wound separation</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Delayed healing</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Scarring</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Altered sensation</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Cosmetic dissatisfaction</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Need for further treatment</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Circumcision permanently removes the foreskin and therefore permanently changes the appearance of the penis.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>We discuss the relevant risks before treatment.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:group --></div><!-- /wp:cil/split --><!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"wp-block-group is-style-cil-callout-urgent body-text\",\"style\":{\"spacing\":{\"margin\":{\"top\":\"36px\"}}}} --><div class=\"wp-block-group is-style-cil-callout-urgent body-text\" style=\"margin-top:36px\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":3,\"className\":\"wp-block-heading display d-3\"} --><h3 class=\"wp-block-heading display d-3\">When should I seek urgent medical attention?</h3><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>BXO itself usually develops gradually rather than presenting as an emergency.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>However, seek urgent medical attention if:</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:list {\"className\":\"cil-checklist\"} --><ul class=\"cil-checklist wp-block-list\"><!-- wp:list-item --><li>You cannot pass urine</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>Your foreskin becomes trapped behind the glans and cannot be returned forward</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>There is rapidly increasing swelling or severe pain</li><!-- /wp:list-item --><!-- wp:list-item --><li>You are seriously unwell with an infection</li><!-- /wp:list-item --></ul><!-- /wp:list --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>You should also arrange appropriate medical assessment for a persistent ulcer, lump, unexplained bleeding or other abnormal area that does not heal.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Do not wait for a routine circumcision appointment if you believe you have an emergency.</strong></p><!-- /wp:paragraph --></div><!-- /wp:group --></div></section><!-- /wp:cil/section -->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/section {\"size\":\"section-sm\",\"band\":\"bg-card edge\",\"wrap\":\"wrap\",\"anchor\":\"pricing\"} --><section class=\"wp-block-cil-section section-sm bg-card edge\" id=\"pricing\"><div class=\"wrap\"><!-- wp:cil/section-head {\"eyebrow\":\"Transparent Pricing\",\"heading\":\"Circumcision fees\",\"display\":\"d-2\"} /--><!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"body-text\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-group body-text\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2,\"className\":\"wp-block-heading display d-2\"} --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading display d-2\">How much does circumcision for BXO cost?</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>At our London clinic, adult circumcision involving a medical or foreskin problem, with or without frenulum treatment, is currently £1,080.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Children with medical foreskin problems are assessed individually.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you believe you have BXO, tell us when contacting the clinic so that we know you are enquiring about circumcision for a medical foreskin condition.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:buttons {\"className\":\"wp-block-buttons\",\"style\":{\"spacing\":{\"margin\":{\"top\":\"20px\"}}}} --><div class=\"wp-block-buttons\" style=\"margin-top:20px\"><!-- wp:button {\"className\":\"wp-block-button\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-button\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"https://circumcision-london-wp.vercel.app/prices/\">View circumcision prices →</a></div><!-- /wp:button --></div><!-- /wp:buttons --></div><!-- /wp:group --></div></section><!-- /wp:cil/section -->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/section {\"size\":\"section\",\"band\":\"\",\"wrap\":\"wrap\",\"anchor\":\"why-choose-us\"} --><section class=\"wp-block-cil-section section\" id=\"why-choose-us\"><div class=\"wrap\"><!-- wp:cil/section-head {\"eyebrow\":\"Clinical Expertise\",\"heading\":\"Why choose Beverley Clinic for BXO circumcision?\",\"display\":\"d-2\"} /--><!-- wp:cil/info-cards {\"columns\":\"g-3\",\"headingLevel\":3,\"items\":[{\"title\":\"Specialist circumcision experience\",\"body\":\"Circumcision is a core part of our day-to-day clinical work rather than an occasional procedure.\"},{\"title\":\"Recognition of scarred foreskin\",\"body\":\"We understand the importance of distinguishing ordinary foreskin tightness from a scarred foreskin where BXO may be present.\"},{\"title\":\"Circumcision planned around the problem\",\"body\":\"With BXO, we identify the location and extent of the abnormal foreskin when planning the circumcision.\"},{\"title\":\"Children, teenagers and adults\",\"body\":\"We provide circumcision across different age groups and adapt the procedure to the patient's age and anatomy.\"},{\"title\":\"Continuity of care\",\"body\":\"Patients receive preparation information before treatment, detailed aftercare afterwards and follow-up during healing.\"}]} /--></div></section><!-- /wp:cil/section -->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/faq {\"heading\":\"Frequently asked questions about BXO\",\"items\":[{\"q\":\"What does BXO stand for?\",\"a\":\"BXO stands for balanitis xerotica obliterans. It is associated with male genital lichen sclerosis.\"},{\"q\":\"Is BXO the same as lichen sclerosis?\",\"a\":\"BXO is the term commonly used when lichen sclerosis affects the male genital area, particularly the foreskin and glans. You may also see the condition spelled “lichen sclerosus” in medical literature.\"},{\"q\":\"What does BXO look like?\",\"a\":\"It can cause white or pale areas, a tight white ring, thickening, loss of elasticity, cracking and scarring of the foreskin.\"},{\"q\":\"Does BXO cause phimosis?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Scarring caused by BXO can narrow the foreskin opening and cause pathological phimosis.\"},{\"q\":\"Can children get BXO?\",\"a\":\"Yes. BXO can affect boys as well as adult men.\"},{\"q\":\"Is BXO an STI?\",\"a\":\"No. BXO is not a sexually transmitted infection and is not contagious.\"},{\"q\":\"Is BXO caused by poor hygiene?\",\"a\":\"No. It is not simply caused by poor personal hygiene.\"},{\"q\":\"What treatment does Beverley Clinic offer for BXO?\",\"a\":\"For BXO affecting the foreskin, the treatment we provide is circumcision.\"},{\"q\":\"Why does BXO circumcision need careful planning?\",\"a\":\"The foreskin can contain a defined area of abnormal, scarred tissue. We assess where this tissue is located so that the circumcision appropriately addresses the problematic foreskin.\"},{\"q\":\"Can BXO affect the urinary opening?\",\"a\":\"Yes. Lichen sclerosis can sometimes affect the opening through which urine passes. A weak, spraying or altered urinary stream should be reported.\"},{\"q\":\"Does circumcision cure BXO?\",\"a\":\"Circumcision removes the affected foreskin and can successfully treat disease limited to the foreskin. BXO can sometimes also affect the glans or urinary opening, so persistent abnormalities after circumcision still require appropriate medical assessment.\"},{\"q\":\"Can I have BXO circumcision under local anaesthetic?\",\"a\":\"Suitable adults at Beverley Clinic are normally circumcised under local anaesthetic.\"},{\"q\":\"When can I have sex after circumcision?\",\"a\":\"Our clinic generally advises avoiding sex and masturbation for at least two weeks and until the wound is sufficiently healed. If healing is incomplete, you should wait longer.\"}]} /-->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/section {\"size\":\"section\",\"band\":\"bg-card edge\",\"wrap\":\"wrap\",\"anchor\":\"book-consultation\"} --><section class=\"wp-block-cil-section section bg-card edge\" id=\"book-consultation\"><div class=\"wrap\"><!-- wp:cil/split {\"reverse\":false} --><div class=\"wp-block-cil-split split\"><!-- wp:group {\"className\":\"body-text\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-group body-text\"><!-- wp:heading {\"level\":2,\"className\":\"wp-block-heading display d-2\"} --><h2 class=\"wp-block-heading display d-2\">Book a consultation for BXO circumcision in London</h2><!-- /wp:heading --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>If you have a white, scarred or progressively tightening foreskin and are considering circumcision, contact Beverley Clinic.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>Tell us if you have been diagnosed with BXO / lichen sclerosis or believe that you may have it.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p>We will examine the foreskin and plan the circumcision according to the extent of the scarring and your individual anatomy.</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:paragraph --><p><strong>Beverley Clinic</strong><br>78 Beverley Drive<br>Edgware<br>North-West London<br>HA8 5NE</p><!-- /wp:paragraph --><!-- wp:buttons {\"className\":\"wp-block-buttons cil-consultation-buttons-grid\",\"style\":{\"spacing\":{\"margin\":{\"top\":\"24px\"}}}} --><div class=\"wp-block-buttons cil-consultation-buttons-grid\" style=\"margin-top:24px\"><!-- wp:button {\"className\":\"wp-block-button is-style-outline\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-button is-style-outline\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"https://circumcision-london-wp.vercel.app/prices/\">View circumcision prices →</a></div><!-- /wp:button --><!-- wp:button {\"className\":\"wp-block-button is-style-outline\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-button is-style-outline\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"https://circumcision-london-wp.vercel.app/contact/\">Contact Beverley Clinic →</a></div><!-- /wp:button --><!-- wp:button {\"className\":\"wp-block-button\"} --><div class=\"wp-block-button\"><a class=\"wp-block-button__link wp-element-button\" href=\"https://circumcision-london-wp.vercel.app/book/\">Book a consultation →</a></div><!-- /wp:button --></div><!-- /wp:buttons --></div><!-- /wp:group --><!-- wp:cil/callback-card {\"eyebrow\":\"Request a call back\",\"title\":\"Ask us first\",\"formId\":\"bxo\",\"subject\":\"BXO / lichen sclerosis enquiry\",\"urgent\":false} /--></div><!-- /wp:cil/split --></div></section><!-- /wp:cil/section -->\n\n<!-- wp:cil/cta-band {\"eyebrow\":\"Talk to us\",\"title\":\"Ask us anything before you decide\",\"text\":\"Most people call with a question rather than to book. That is what the phone is for, and nothing is booked until you say so.\",\"ctaLabel\":\"Book a consultation\",\"ctaUrl\":\"https://circumcision-london-wp.vercel.app/book/\",\"phoneLabel\":\"Call 020 8951 3794\",\"phoneUrl\":\"tel:+442089513794\"} /-->"
